<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aitchison College</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Punjab Chiefs' College, Wards School</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aitchison College is a day and boarding school for boys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> Chiefs' College on 2 January 1886 and renamed after the Lieutenant Governor of the Punjab, Sir Charles Umpherston Aitchison, that November. It grew out of the Wards School at Ambala, established in 1868 to educate young princes and widened in 1874 to the heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the Punjab. Admission was opened to ordinary boys from the surrounding areas in 1935. The college has since educated prime ministers of Pakistan, among them Imran Khan, as well as presidents, lawyers and cricketers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aitchison-college.jpg" alt="Aitchison College" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mianhammad59 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aitchisoncollege.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Princes who had kept out of the rebellion of 1857 had their territories guaranteed, and their sons were schooled at Mayo College and <b>Aitchison College</b> on the model of the English public schools.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
